WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.780 --> 00:00:18.660
Tim Ferriss: Hello, boys and girls, ladies and&nbsp;
germs. This is Tim Ferriss. Welcome to another&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.660 --> 00:00:22.920
episode of The Tim Ferriss Show, where it is&nbsp;
my job to deconstruct world class performers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.920 --> 00:00:28.440
from all different walks of life, all different&nbsp;
arenas, to tease out the habits, lessons learned,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.440 --> 00:00:34.020
et cetera, that you can apply to your own lives.
My guest today, long overdue, is Mark Manson. You&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.020 --> 00:00:39.680
can find him all over the webs, including&nbsp;
on Twitter @IAmMarkManson. Mark is a three&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.680 --> 00:00:44.220
time number-one New York Times bestselling&nbsp;
author of The Subtle Art of Not Giving a Fuck,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.220 --> 00:00:50.760
maybe you've heard of it as well as other titles.&nbsp;
His books have sold nearly two zero, 20 million&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.760 --> 00:00:55.980
copies worldwide, and have reached number one in&nbsp;
more than a dozen countries. Since 2008, he has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.980 --> 00:01:00.960
written articles at MarkManson.net, one of the&nbsp;
largest personal development blogs in the world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.960 --> 00:01:06.540
with more than a million monthly readers. In&nbsp;
2023, a feature film about him and his work&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.540 --> 00:01:10.740
will be released in theaters by Universal&nbsp;
Pictures. We'll link to everything in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.740 --> 00:01:16.440
show notes as per usual at tim.blog/podcast.&nbsp;
Mark, welcome to the show. Nice to see you.&nbsp;

00:01:16.440 --> 00:01:22.980
Mark Manson: It's a pleasure. Happy to be here.
Tim Ferriss: I read this profile of you in New&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.980 --> 00:01:29.460
York Mag, and it shared many different stories and&nbsp;
I want to dig into maybe certain aspects of that.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.120 --> 00:01:34.980
But what I'd be curious to hear, just off the&nbsp;
cuff, because a lot has happened in your life&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.980 --> 00:01:42.540
in the last five to seven years, what have&nbsp;
been some of the most difficult changes to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.540 --> 00:01:48.960
get accustomed to or default behaviors to&nbsp;
modify now that your reality has changed?&nbsp;

00:01:49.500 --> 00:01:57.000
Mark Manson: I feel like we could do the&nbsp;
entire show just on this. You could do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.000 --> 00:02:01.500
probably do an entire episode. Maybe you&nbsp;
could get an all-star guest list of what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.500 --> 00:02:05.613
people don't tell you about success.
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, let's do it.&nbsp;

00:02:05.613 --> 00:02:09.180
Mark Manson: So one thing, the most surprising&nbsp;
thing and the most difficult thing at the same&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.180 --> 00:02:16.920
time was The Subtle Art of not Giving a Fuck, came&nbsp;
out in 2016, it hit number one early 2017. And by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.920 --> 00:02:22.860
mid 2017 it was just off to the races. I think it&nbsp;
sold two or three million copies just that year.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.140 --> 00:02:29.340
And for me, it was very strange. I mean obviously&nbsp;
there was a lot of happiness and joy, but for me,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.340 --> 00:02:34.320
it was very strange, because as a young author&nbsp;
with their first traditionally published book,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.320 --> 00:02:40.680
I had all of these long-term goals and dreams&nbsp;
and visions for myself of I'm going to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.680 --> 00:02:46.440
writing till I'm 50, and maybe one day I'll be&nbsp;
on the Times list and maybe one day I'll get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.440 --> 00:02:53.340
invited on a morning show. And I hit all&nbsp;
those dreams in three months, basically.&nbsp;

00:02:54.120 --> 00:02:59.100
And so my surprise is that there was&nbsp;
just this profound kind of emptiness&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.100 --> 00:03:04.920
on the other side of that, of, "Oh, what do I&nbsp;
dream now? What do I hope for in my future?"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.060 --> 00:03:12.960
And so very unexpectedly, I spiraled into a little&nbsp;
bit of a funk, a little bit of a depression. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.960 --> 00:03:17.387
the worst thing about that type of depression&nbsp;
is nobody wants to hear you talk about it.&nbsp;

00:03:17.387 --> 00:03:22.860
Tim Ferriss: There's no tears shed.
Mark Manson: Yes, no sympathy whatsoever.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.860 --> 00:03:27.040
Like, "Oh, boohoo, poor author, sold&nbsp;
three million books," or whatever. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.660 --> 00:03:35.640
so it really caught me off guard. And there was a&nbsp;
little bit, there was imposter syndrome, which I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.640 --> 00:03:41.520
think comes with any sort of monumental success.&nbsp;
There was a little bit of what do I do now?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.200 --> 00:03:47.760
How do I follow this up? A lot of insecurity of&nbsp;
am I ever going to do anything this meaningful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.760 --> 00:03:53.940
or noteworthy again? And I struggled with&nbsp;
that for a number of years, and that was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.940 --> 00:04:01.260
just on the emotional and personal side. That&nbsp;
doesn't even get into relationships and money.&nbsp;

00:04:01.260 --> 00:04:10.200
I feel like it's almost like a prerequisite&nbsp;
to being "wealthy" is you have to spend money&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.200 --> 00:04:15.420
on something stupid that you regret. I just feel&nbsp;
like that is a non-negotiable. You're not really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.420 --> 00:04:20.207
wealthy until you've done that at some point.&nbsp;
So I went through a couple of those experiences.&nbsp;

00:04:20.207 --> 00:04:23.520
Tim Ferriss: What would be the most notable?
Mark Manson: A fucking house.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.000 --> 00:04:33.120
The funny thing about me is I'm very&nbsp;
minimalist. I'm very Spartan. I don't really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.780 --> 00:04:37.260
like to have a lot of possessions, but for&nbsp;
some reason when it was time to buy a house,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.260 --> 00:04:42.780
I'm like, "You know what? Let's just get all&nbsp;
the rooms. I have nothing to put in them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.780 --> 00:04:48.300
but let's get them all." And that&nbsp;
was just an unbelievable mistake that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.260 --> 00:04:54.240
really made my life a headache for a while.
Tim Ferriss: What are some of the things — and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.240 --> 00:05:00.240
just so people listening understand where I'm&nbsp;
probably going with this, there is a meandering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.240 --> 00:05:04.680
path, but there is a path, is I want to talk&nbsp;
about the present tense and then I'm going to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.680 --> 00:05:11.640
rewind and do, I don't want to call it a life&nbsp;
review, but look at some of the trajectory at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.640 --> 00:05:18.600
least insofar as I've been able to observe it in&nbsp;
the course of watching you do what you've done&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.260 --> 00:05:24.060
from the sidelines, but also having done research&nbsp;
for the conversation. So present tense though,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.060 --> 00:05:28.380
building on what we were just talking about. What&nbsp;
are some of the things you thought you wanted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.340 --> 00:05:33.720
or needed that you got and you were like, "Huh?"&nbsp;

00:05:34.500 --> 00:05:42.180
Mark Manson: I would say actually the biggest&nbsp;
thing, and this is kind of a broad thing, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.240 --> 00:05:51.240
I think you and I are very similar in that&nbsp;
we started as online blog guys. And I think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.240 --> 00:05:56.940
when you come up in the internet world,&nbsp;
even if you amass a very large audience,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.940 --> 00:06:01.800
at least for me, there was always a&nbsp;
little bit of this chip on my shoulder of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.940 --> 00:06:11.220
I'm not a real writer. I don't have an article&nbsp;
in a magazine. There hasn't been a profile on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.220 --> 00:06:17.340
me in a newspaper. And there seemed to be kind&nbsp;
of an invisible wall between me and that world.&nbsp;

00:06:17.340 --> 00:06:24.540
And so for a number of years I really, really&nbsp;
wanted that, for lack of a better term,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.540 --> 00:06:30.120
mainstream media attention. Just that nod&nbsp;
of respect of like, "Yeah, kid, you did it."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.120 --> 00:06:40.560
And when I started getting that and started&nbsp;
working and engaging with a lot of legacy media,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.660 --> 00:06:49.800
I was like, "Wow, this sucks. I miss my&nbsp;
blog. I miss the emails from my readers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.800 --> 00:06:53.340
I don't want to do this. This is such&nbsp;
a pain in the ass." We have Zoom calls&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.340 --> 00:06:57.540
with 11 people on them, and I don't know&nbsp;
who half the people are. So that's been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.540 --> 00:07:02.940
a pretty eye-opening thing the last few years.
Tim Ferriss: And to flesh out the current state,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.780 --> 00:07:09.420
most people listening will have heard&nbsp;
about Universal Pictures or have some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.420 --> 00:07:15.840
passing familiarity, "Oh, maybe I saw that at the&nbsp;
beginning of a movie, I think." Yes, you did. Not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.840 --> 00:07:25.800
a small entity. And I would love to know how this&nbsp;
feature film came to be. How did it come together?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.800 --> 00:07:33.540
And related to that, what does your current&nbsp;
team look like? Not only including any full-time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.540 --> 00:07:39.600
employees, but also agents, managers, et cetera.&nbsp;
Just what does that constellation look like?&nbsp;

00:07:39.600 --> 00:07:42.960
Mark Manson: Well, the film, I&nbsp;
kind of Forrest Gumped my way into.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.220 --> 00:07:49.607
After the book blew up a bunch of studios&nbsp;
started sending offers to my agent and —&nbsp;

00:07:49.607 --> 00:07:54.480
Tim Ferriss: For sure. To your book agent.
Mark Manson: Yes. To my book agent. And I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.480 --> 00:08:00.960
know you know this, but for everybody else, it's&nbsp;
pretty common for film and TV studios to buy up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.960 --> 00:08:06.780
rights to bestselling books without any intention&nbsp;
of actually doing anything with them. It's kind of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.780 --> 00:08:12.540
just like a land grab. Just in case, so-and-so&nbsp;
director one day is like, "I love that book.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.540 --> 00:08:19.680
I want to do a thing with it." They already own&nbsp;
it. So out of all these offers that came through,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.300 --> 00:08:26.400
there was some pretty off the wall stuff. There&nbsp;
was some stuff that I couldn't ever imagine myself&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.400 --> 00:08:31.440
doing. And I told my agent, I said, "If we do&nbsp;
any of these deals, it's really important — the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.440 --> 00:08:35.700
only reason I think I'm ever going to do a film&nbsp;
or a movie or film or a TV show is to simply&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.700 --> 00:08:40.740
spread these same ideas to people who don't read&nbsp;
books. To me, that's the only reason to do this."&nbsp;

00:08:41.760 --> 00:08:47.160
So when the offer for a documentary came&nbsp;
through from a production company that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.160 --> 00:08:50.640
specializes in documentaries and has done&nbsp;
a bunch of them based on books before,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.300 --> 00:08:57.360
to me, it just seemed like a very logical fit.&nbsp;
I was like, "Okay, if I was to ever have a movie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.360 --> 00:09:04.380
that is the kind of project that I would do. I&nbsp;
would feel good about." My agent told me, she&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.380 --> 00:09:09.660
said, "These things never get made. Don't get your&nbsp;
hopes up." I was like, "Okay, cool. I've never had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:09.660 --> 00:09:14.100
any ambition to have a movie or be in the movies."&nbsp;
So I was like, "Cool," kind of forgot about it.&nbsp;

00:09:14.880 --> 00:09:17.460
Like a year and a half goes&nbsp;
by and I get a phone call&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.120 --> 00:09:22.680
and it's the production company, and they're like,&nbsp;
"Guess what? We got financed by Universal." And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.680 --> 00:09:27.420
I was like, "Okay, what does that mean?" And&nbsp;
they're like, "You're going to have a movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.420 --> 00:09:35.460
you idiot. Do you want to be in it?" And I was&nbsp;
like, "Sure, why not?" And then from there,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.460 --> 00:09:38.700
next thing I know, I'm on a plane. I'm flying down&nbsp;
the New Zealand. I'm meeting with the production&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.700 --> 00:09:41.880
company. I'm meeting with directors.
Tim Ferriss: Was New Zealand due to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.880 --> 00:09:46.680
COVID? Is that why it was in New Zealand?
Mark Manson: Yes, it was. The production&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.680 --> 00:09:50.880
company is actually based in New Zealand, but&nbsp;
we were originally going to do it in New York.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.720 --> 00:09:56.700
But then when COVID happened, I used it also as an&nbsp;
opportunity to just get the hell away from COVID.&nbsp;

00:09:56.700 --> 00:10:00.240
So during this whole process, they're telling me,&nbsp;
"We're going to have a theatrical release. We're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.240 --> 00:10:04.500
going to take it to festivals. We're going to do&nbsp;
this. We're going to do that." And I don't know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.140 --> 00:10:11.160
and I'm sure we're going to talk about Will&nbsp;
later, but maybe from being around Will, I just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:11.160 --> 00:10:16.560
became jaded of Hollywood people. I was just like,&nbsp;
"Yeah, sure," while not really believing it. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:17.400 --> 00:10:21.360
sure enough, we're a month out and it's&nbsp;
coming out, and it's going to have a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.360 --> 00:10:23.520
limited theatrical release. It's&nbsp;
going to be available online.&nbsp;

00:10:25.560 --> 00:10:32.040
And I seem to be the one who's like, "Wait,&nbsp;
this is actually happening." It's one of those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:32.040 --> 00:10:37.020
projects that I can't take a ton of credit&nbsp;
for consciously navigating myself into. I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:37.020 --> 00:10:43.187
don't have the three bullet points of how&nbsp;
to get your movie made. I wish I did, but —&nbsp;

00:10:43.187 --> 00:10:48.900
Tim Ferriss: Step one, have an&nbsp;
unexpected global bestseller.&nbsp;

00:10:48.900 --> 00:10:54.780
Mark Manson: Yes. Step one, sell 10 million books.
Tim Ferriss: Step two, wait for a lot of inbound.&nbsp;

00:10:57.720 --> 00:11:01.860
Mark Manson: Not very reproducible.&nbsp;
In terms of your question about team,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:03.240 --> 00:11:10.320
so I have my agent at CAA and she's phenomenal.&nbsp;
She's amazing. And the amazing thing about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:10.320 --> 00:11:17.700
her being at CAA is that it opens up all&nbsp;
these crossover opportunities into film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:17.700 --> 00:11:23.880
into TV. It's how I ended up doing Will's book,&nbsp;
or a big part of why I ended up doing Will's book.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:25.980 --> 00:11:31.140
So she's great. I have my web team,&nbsp;
which is four full-time people.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:32.400 --> 00:11:40.080
I have a researcher. I have a content guy.&nbsp;
That is his official title, content guy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:40.080 --> 00:11:48.000
because he just wears a lot of hats and he kind&nbsp;
of makes everything run. I have a tech developer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:48.960 --> 00:11:54.840
tech/development guy, and then I have a social&nbsp;
media manager and it's all in-house. Everything&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:54.840 --> 00:12:00.060
we do is in-house. All the design, the tech&nbsp;
stuff, the promotion, the marketing, everything.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:01.860 --> 00:12:05.580
We use a few freelancers, if we're doing&nbsp;
video content, stuff like that. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:07.080 --> 00:12:09.900
yeah, that's the team right now.
Tim Ferriss: Number of follow-up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:09.900 --> 00:12:13.187
questions on this, the web team,&nbsp;
you said four full-time people.&nbsp;

00:12:13.187 --> 00:12:14.940
Mark Manson: Yep.
Tim Ferriss: Now is that including&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:14.940 --> 00:12:20.820
the researcher, content guy, tech, and social?&nbsp;
Or is that in addition to, so you end up having&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:21.780 --> 00:12:24.780
seven or eight full-time folks?
Mark Manson: No, those are the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:24.780 --> 00:12:27.060
four positions that are full-time.
Tim Ferriss: I see. I see. Got&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:27.060 --> 00:12:30.900
it. Got it. What does the researcher&nbsp;
do? I mean, I know they do research,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:30.900 --> 00:12:33.840
but what type of research?
Mark Manson: So I don't have a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:33.840 --> 00:12:40.980
graduate degree in psychology, yet, I consistently&nbsp;
find myself in the sticky situation of having to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:40.980 --> 00:12:45.347
say something that feels somewhat authoritative. I&nbsp;
mean, I'm sure you're in this position a lot too.&nbsp;

00:12:45.347 --> 00:12:47.760
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, sure.
Mark Manson: At a certain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:47.760 --> 00:12:51.420
point I just went out and hired people with&nbsp;
graduate degrees in psychology, and said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:51.420 --> 00:12:56.940
"Look, can you just make sure I'm not putting my&nbsp;
foot in my mouth or giving terrible advice?" So a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:56.940 --> 00:13:02.700
lot of times I'll go to her and I'll say,&nbsp;
"I've been thinking about an article on,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:04.680 --> 00:13:09.300
say, the negative side effects of self-esteem. Can&nbsp;
you go out there, find all the research and data,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:09.300 --> 00:13:12.480
what are the studies? Are they&nbsp;
good studies? Were they done well?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:14.160 --> 00:13:19.080
And then just summarize it for me." And then&nbsp;
if I want to dig into it with her, I can.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:19.800 --> 00:13:24.240
She's been invaluable. It's almost&nbsp;
like outsourcing a piece of my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:24.240 --> 00:13:29.940
brain to somebody. And it's been very helpful.
Tim Ferriss: And I'm asking about the team&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:30.840 --> 00:13:36.360
out of personal curiosity of course,&nbsp;
but also because I enjoy and benefit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:36.360 --> 00:13:40.140
from hearing how people think about teams.&nbsp;
For instance, I'll give you an example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:41.340 --> 00:13:44.880
Kevin Kelly, arguably the most&nbsp;
interesting man in the world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:44.880 --> 00:13:51.120
people should look him up, kk.org. Also,&nbsp;
the author of a famous article called&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:51.120 --> 00:13:55.860
"1,000 True Fans," which I encourage&nbsp;
everybody to read. And he said to me once,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:56.760 --> 00:14:01.680
explained to me rather that he has an assistant&nbsp;
and then he has a researcher, but they're not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:01.680 --> 00:14:06.480
the same person. And he said in his experience&nbsp;
very often to optimize for one or the other,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:08.340 --> 00:14:12.720
you will find that they are somewhat&nbsp;
mutually exclusive. In other words,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:12.720 --> 00:14:16.200
the traits are somewhat mutually exclusive.
Now, I don't know if I agree or disagree with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:16.200 --> 00:14:22.860
that, but it was an interesting observation based&nbsp;
on the pattern recognition from someone I respect.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:23.760 --> 00:14:31.980
So how did you choose full-time versus&nbsp;
contractor? Or internal versus external&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:31.980 --> 00:14:36.360
would be another way to put it, I suppose.
Mark Manson: Some of this is personal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:36.360 --> 00:14:43.680
preference. I like working long-term with&nbsp;
people. I like finding exceptional talent,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:45.660 --> 00:14:49.920
paying them very well, and then sticking with&nbsp;
them over a long period of time. So I've never&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:49.920 --> 00:14:57.540
had an employee leave. And two of my guys, one's&nbsp;
been with me for 10 years, one's been with me for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:57.540 --> 00:15:03.660
almost nine, and then the other two have been with&nbsp;
me for about three or four, which in the web world&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:03.660 --> 00:15:06.943
is crazy. That's like crazy talk.
Tim Ferriss: It's eternity.&nbsp;

00:15:06.943 --> 00:15:10.860
Mark Manson: And I have entrepreneur friends&nbsp;
with online businesses and they look at me&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:10.860 --> 00:15:14.880
and they're like, "Are you nuts? You could&nbsp;
pay these people half as much." I'm like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:14.880 --> 00:15:20.400
"Yeah, but I'd be hiring every six months."
Tim Ferriss: It'd be a Faustian bargain. Yeah,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:20.400 --> 00:15:24.420
you'd be paying for it with your time, for sure.
Mark Manson: Exactly. And I also just,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:24.420 --> 00:15:30.960
there's something to be said with the trust&nbsp;
and loyalty. I don't know how you measure that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:30.960 --> 00:15:37.140
but there's the trust and loyalty that comes&nbsp;
with working with somebody for 10 years. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:39.180 --> 00:15:44.520
two days before we're recording this, we&nbsp;
completely redesigned my site and relaunched&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:44.520 --> 00:15:53.220
my newsletter. It was a multi-month project, and&nbsp;
I know the guys that I have, I know I don't have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:53.220 --> 00:15:58.200
to look over their shoulder. I know they're going&nbsp;
to get it done. I know it's going to be well done.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:59.220 --> 00:16:03.480
And a lot of that is simply because&nbsp;
I've been with them for so long and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:03.480 --> 00:16:06.660
they've been with me for so long.
Tim Ferriss: I feel the same way,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:06.660 --> 00:16:12.180
which is why I have full-time team. In addition to&nbsp;
contractors, I do use freelancers and contractors.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:12.180 --> 00:16:20.280
How did you find the members of your team? And we&nbsp;
don't necessarily have to go one by one, but do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:20.280 --> 00:16:29.640
you have a particular approach to sourcing talent?
Mark Manson: Yes. So I always find them through my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:29.640 --> 00:16:38.280
email list and my subscribers. I've tried through&nbsp;
kind of conventional job searching venues, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:41.940 --> 00:16:45.660
you get so much extra leverage from, I guess,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:45.660 --> 00:16:52.380
the fan relationship. Generally, every time I&nbsp;
ask for applications through my own audience,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:53.340 --> 00:17:01.140
the people who apply are almost comically&nbsp;
overqualified and the talent level is through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:01.140 --> 00:17:05.100
the roof. And a lot of that is just, they love&nbsp;
my work, they've been reading it for years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:06.660 --> 00:17:15.540
and that earns you a lot of clout.
Tim Ferriss: It does. That makes sense. And do you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:16.200 --> 00:17:20.100
mail out a link to a page with a job&nbsp;
description? Do you just simply put&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:20.100 --> 00:17:23.040
it at the end of one of your newsletters.&nbsp;
You're like, "Hey, by the way, I'm looking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:23.040 --> 00:17:28.380
for this." Is it dedicated? How do you do it?
Mark Manson: So I will create a temporary page&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:28.380 --> 00:17:34.800
for the position. I usually go through three&nbsp;
steps. So I have the initial application,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:34.800 --> 00:17:41.040
it asks for the generic stuff, resume,&nbsp;
cover letter. I'll usually ask a few cute&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:41.040 --> 00:17:48.540
questions in there. What are your three favorite&nbsp;
books? What was the last risk that you took,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:48.540 --> 00:17:52.440
major risk you took in your life, and&nbsp;
what happened? Things like that. Although,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:54.120 --> 00:17:58.620
as the years have gone on, I'm discovering that&nbsp;
those questions aren't really great filters.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:00.600 --> 00:18:05.160
So I'll do that. That's kind of the first round.&nbsp;
I'll get hundreds and hundreds of applications,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:05.160 --> 00:18:12.540
and then I narrow it down to say 20 to 30 people.
Tim Ferriss: Do you review all of those yourself?&nbsp;

00:18:12.540 --> 00:18:19.436
Mark Manson: So sometimes I will have a team&nbsp;
member kind of go through and just be anything —&nbsp;

00:18:19.436 --> 00:18:22.680
Tim Ferriss: Putting highlights.
Mark Manson: Yeah. Or anything that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:22.680 --> 00:18:27.600
doesn't — because I'll set standards on the&nbsp;
application page. So I'll tell them, "Anything&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:27.600 --> 00:18:33.000
that doesn't meet the application standards just&nbsp;
kick out." So this most recent round of hire,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:33.000 --> 00:18:39.120
I'm hiring a video team to start doing more video&nbsp;
content, and I asked for at least three years of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:39.120 --> 00:18:45.120
professional full-time experience in some form&nbsp;
of whether it's TV, film, YouTube, whatever.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:45.840 --> 00:18:51.000
And sure enough, you get 200 applications from&nbsp;
people who have never held a camera before,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:51.000 --> 00:18:58.740
but they're really ready to learn. They can't wait&nbsp;
to learn, Tim. So it's nice to have somebody to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:58.740 --> 00:19:02.340
just go and mark those all red for me.
Tim Ferriss: They want a scholarship&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:02.340 --> 00:19:08.520
ride to graduate school at Manson University.
Mark Manson: Exactly. Exactly. So I'll filter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:08.520 --> 00:19:14.340
those down to 20 to 30 strong candidates,&nbsp;
people who meet the requirements and seem&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:14.340 --> 00:19:19.260
smart and talented and driven.&nbsp;
And then I'll actually give them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:20.340 --> 00:19:27.900
a few small pieces of work to do, like, example&nbsp;
tasks similar to what the position is hiring for.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:28.920 --> 00:19:33.900
And I'll give them a deadline to meet it at.&nbsp;
And it's always funny, because there's always&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:33.900 --> 00:19:41.160
at least two or three people who immediately&nbsp;
complain about this. And they say, "This is slave&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:41.160 --> 00:19:46.500
labor. You're taking advantage of us. You're&nbsp;
using us to crowdsource ideas for yourself."&nbsp;

00:19:46.500 --> 00:19:53.760
Which is silly, because, I mean, I understand&nbsp;
why they think that, but I think anybody who is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:53.760 --> 00:19:58.980
in our industry understands that ideas&nbsp;
aren't the thing that matter. And so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:00.960 --> 00:20:05.340
from that, I'll narrow it down to three to&nbsp;
five people, and then those people I'll do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:06.000 --> 00:20:11.280
face-to-face Zoom interviews, until I find&nbsp;
the one that feels like the right fit.&nbsp;

00:20:11.280 --> 00:20:13.620
Tim Ferriss: What are you&nbsp;
looking for in the interviews?&nbsp;

00:20:13.620 --> 00:20:18.300
Mark Manson: So by the time they get to the&nbsp;
interview, it is, they've basically shown&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:18.300 --> 00:20:22.380
that they are completely qualified to do the&nbsp;
work. They're probably going to do a great job.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:23.040 --> 00:20:30.000
The interviews for me, it's 99 percent looking for&nbsp;
intangibles, looking for personality, character.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:31.080 --> 00:20:38.580
I usually try to get a sense of what their values&nbsp;
are, get a sense of how resilient they are.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:40.320 --> 00:20:44.640
Do they take responsibility for themselves?&nbsp;
A little bit about their background,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:44.640 --> 00:20:48.300
things like that. But also just like, do I get&nbsp;
along with them? Do I like talking to them?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:50.580 --> 00:20:52.980
Do we have a sense of humor that meshes well?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:54.900 --> 00:20:58.500
Things like that.
Tim Ferriss: Makes sense. Last question on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:58.500 --> 00:21:04.380
the team. And there's a point to all this, people&nbsp;
listening, part of which is just satisfying my own&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:04.380 --> 00:21:13.740
interest, big part of which. But CAA, this agent,&nbsp;
there are a lot of agents and you've got quite a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:13.740 --> 00:21:20.040
few three letter acronyms to choose from, right?&nbsp;
You've got CAA, WME, UTA, and there are many other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:20.040 --> 00:21:25.500
boutique agencies and so on. A lot of people out&nbsp;
there who could ostensibly probably do a pretty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:25.500 --> 00:21:31.740
good job. Many who would promise the moon and&nbsp;
do a terrible job. But how specifically did you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:32.580 --> 00:21:37.500
decide on this agent that you work with at CAA?
Mark Manson: That's actually a great question.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:37.500 --> 00:21:46.560
So my blog blew up in 2013, 2014. It kind of&nbsp;
went from maybe a hundred thousand readers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:46.560 --> 00:21:54.480
to a million, million and a half. And around&nbsp;
that time, agents and publishers started coming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:54.480 --> 00:22:00.780
knocking, asking if I wanted to turn some of&nbsp;
my work into a book. And at that time, again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:01.380 --> 00:22:07.740
homegrown internet guy. I self-published my first&nbsp;
book. I've been selling online courses since 2010.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:09.420 --> 00:22:14.520
I've always had a little bit of an&nbsp;
irrational, I want to do it myself&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:14.520 --> 00:22:19.440
mentality. But back then I had a very, very&nbsp;
irrational, I want to do it myself mentality.&nbsp;

00:22:19.440 --> 00:22:25.740
So a lot of my calls with these editors and&nbsp;
agents was basically me just being a bit of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:25.740 --> 00:22:30.720
dick and being like, "Why should I publish a book&nbsp;
at all? Why shouldn't I just self-publish. I get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:30.720 --> 00:22:38.040
way more royalties or whatever?" And it was funny,&nbsp;
because pretty much everybody in the publishing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:38.040 --> 00:22:42.060
industry kind of stumbled over that question.&nbsp;
They couldn't really give me a satisfying answer.&nbsp;

00:22:43.800 --> 00:22:49.980
So my agent, her name's Mollie, at the time,&nbsp;
she wasn't at CAA, she was at a small boutique&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:49.980 --> 00:22:54.420
agency in New York. So I get on the phone&nbsp;
with her and we start having a conversation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:54.420 --> 00:23:00.420
and she's constantly interrupting me, she's&nbsp;
constantly talking over me. She's very aggressive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:00.420 --> 00:23:07.140
and assertive, and to the point that I'm getting&nbsp;
a little bit uncomfortable talking to her. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:07.140 --> 00:23:14.280
so I throw out my gotcha question, "Why should I&nbsp;
even publish a book at all? I can self-publish a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:14.280 --> 00:23:19.260
book and keep all the royalties for myself?" And&nbsp;
she kind of sat there silently, and she was like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:20.100 --> 00:23:24.240
"Well, figure out what the hell you want&nbsp;
to do. Stop wasting my time. Call me if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:24.240 --> 00:23:30.060
you want to publish a book," and hung up on me.
And I was like, "Goddamn, all right." And a couple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:30.060 --> 00:23:36.900
hours go by and I start thinking to myself, I'm&nbsp;
like, "That's who I want bargaining on my behalf.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:36.900 --> 00:23:46.140
If I have a six or seven figure negotiation with&nbsp;
somebody, I want her on my team." So I called&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:46.140 --> 00:23:54.180
her back and I said, "I'm in." And she's a total&nbsp;
rockstar in her field. And it was, again, Forrest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:54.180 --> 00:24:00.300
Gump moment. So right before Subtle Art came out,&nbsp;
she called me and she said, "Hey, I just want to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:00.300 --> 00:24:06.540
let you know I'm moving to a new agency. Nothing's&nbsp;
going to change. I'm going over to CAA." And I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:06.540 --> 00:24:13.200
was like, "Oh, cool, what's CAA?" And she kind of&nbsp;
thought I was joking, I guess, she didn't answer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:13.200 --> 00:24:19.200
me. And I remember, I googled CAA and if you go to&nbsp;
their website, it just has a snail mail address.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:19.200 --> 00:24:22.800
There's literally nothing on their website.
Tim Ferriss: There's nothing. Yeah. So for folks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:22.800 --> 00:24:26.340
wondering if they don't know, it's Creative&nbsp;
Artists Agency, it's one of the largest,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:27.120 --> 00:24:31.727
suppose we could call it, talent agencies&nbsp;
in the world. They represent a lot of the —&nbsp;

00:24:31.727 --> 00:24:33.840
Mark Manson: Everybody.
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, top tier,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:34.920 --> 00:24:40.380
A grade celebrities you would recognize.
Mark Manson: And it was maybe a week later,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:40.380 --> 00:24:45.420
I was talking to my editor and he&nbsp;
goes, "Hey, congrats." And I was like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:45.420 --> 00:24:49.620
"For what?" And he goes, "Mollie's&nbsp;
moving to CAA." I was like, "Yeah,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:49.620 --> 00:24:53.400
why are you telling me congrats?" And he just&nbsp;
looked at me like I was the biggest fucking idiot.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:56.280 --> 00:25:01.620
He's like, "Manson, you just won the lottery."
Tim Ferriss: Well, speaking of winning the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:01.620 --> 00:25:06.960
lottery, let's talk about, and this is going&nbsp;
to be a bit of a strained metaphor, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:08.220 --> 00:25:13.260
putting yourself in a position where at least&nbsp;
you have a chance at winning the lottery. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:13.260 --> 00:25:21.960
I'm going to further confuse matters by reading a&nbsp;
paragraph from a Wikipedia entry on a video game,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:21.960 --> 00:25:24.600
and this will hopefully make some&nbsp;
sense for people in a second.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:25.740 --> 00:25:31.560
There's a video game called, and I don't know how&nbsp;
it's pronounced in English actually, but Katamari&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:31.560 --> 00:25:39.240
Damacy in English, but in Japanese, it's Katamari&nbsp;
Damashii, which literally means clump spirit.&nbsp;

00:25:39.240 --> 00:25:44.880
And the reason I learned about this video game was&nbsp;
in a presentation by Matt Cutts, who at the time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:45.540 --> 00:25:51.480
was at Google and he was giving a presentation,&nbsp;
I think it was at the State of the Word,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:51.480 --> 00:25:57.240
or an annual WordPress meetup. I could be&nbsp;
getting those specifics wrong, but I was there.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:57.840 --> 00:26:04.260
And he described how he observed&nbsp;
people building audiences over time.&nbsp;

00:26:04.800 --> 00:26:09.840
This is where we're going to go through the&nbsp;
looking glass and go back in time with your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:09.840 --> 00:26:15.480
story. But here's the plot, this is directly&nbsp;
from Wikipedia. “The game’s plot concerns&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:15.480 --> 00:26:19.980
a diminutive prince.” I'm not saying you're a&nbsp;
diminutive prince. “The game’s plot concerns a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:19.980 --> 00:26:23.340
diminutive prince on a mission to rebuild the&nbsp;
stars, constellations, and moon, which were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:23.340 --> 00:26:28.440
inadvertently destroyed by his father, the King of&nbsp;
All Cosmos. This is achieved by rolling a magical,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:28.440 --> 00:26:33.900
highly adhesive ball called a katamari around&nbsp;
various locations, collecting increasingly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:33.900 --> 00:26:38.940
larger objects, this is the key part, ranging from&nbsp;
thumbtacks to human beings to mountains, until the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:38.940 --> 00:26:44.400
ball has grown large enough to become a star,”&nbsp;
goes on and on. So that's Katamari Damashii,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:44.400 --> 00:26:48.660
at least in Japanese, I suspect they changed the&nbsp;
spelling D-A-M-A-C-Y, so they could trademark&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:48.660 --> 00:26:52.140
it. But that's a side note.
All right, Matt's point was,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:53.100 --> 00:27:00.780
when you first start, you are generally, in these&nbsp;
success stories he has seen, starting very small&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:00.780 --> 00:27:04.380
with a ball that's rolling up thumbtacks, and&nbsp;
then eventually it gets a little bit bigger&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:04.380 --> 00:27:08.700
and you move to larger objects, and then you get&nbsp;
to humans, then you get to mountains and so on.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:09.360 --> 00:27:17.220
And I thought maybe we could start, if you're&nbsp;
open to it with you telling the backstory on a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:17.220 --> 00:27:26.243
character, maybe not a character, a person named&nbsp;
Entropy. Could you explain who Entropy is or was?&nbsp;

00:27:26.243 --> 00:27:28.200
Mark Manson: Oh, my God.
Tim Ferriss: And then walk us&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:28.200 --> 00:27:34.020
through the development, because it seems to&nbsp;
parallel this video game. In actually a very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:34.020 --> 00:27:39.480
similar way that my trajectory has&nbsp;
mirrored this video game in a sense,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:39.480 --> 00:27:47.100
the metaphor at least. So Entropy, who is Entropy?
Mark Manson: Oh, God. Well, ironically enough, it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:47.100 --> 00:27:53.880
was originally my gaming handle when I was in high&nbsp;
school, but it was my, I guess, you could call&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:53.880 --> 00:28:01.380
it a pseudonym of my first blog. I started&nbsp;
blogging, my roommate had a blog in college,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:02.220 --> 00:28:08.940
and he and I, the pickup artist thing was&nbsp;
popular at the time, and so there was a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:08.940 --> 00:28:15.840
lot of kind of writing, like, "Oh, I went on a&nbsp;
date last weekend, this is what happened." And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:18.000 --> 00:28:23.460
him and another friend of mine were kind of always&nbsp;
egging me to, "Start a blog, dude. Start a blog."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:23.460 --> 00:28:28.920
And it was really just because we all read each&nbsp;
other's stories and stuff, and they wanted to read&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:28.920 --> 00:28:36.660
mine. So that's actually how it first started,&nbsp;
was just my roommate peer pressuring me into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:36.660 --> 00:28:41.340
sharing stupid drunk things I did, as a —
Tim Ferriss: And what year is this roughly?&nbsp;

00:28:41.340 --> 00:28:44.923
Mark Manson: This was 2007.
Tim Ferriss: 2007. Okay.&nbsp;

00:28:44.923 --> 00:28:54.960
Mark Manson: — I was 23, and doing things at&nbsp;
23-year-olds do. And again, Forrest Gumping my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:54.960 --> 00:29:03.060
way into it, at that stage, there was no ambition&nbsp;
or kind of vision for this. It's just the cool&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:03.060 --> 00:29:08.760
thing that my friends want me to do. And I enjoyed&nbsp;
it too, I found. That was kind of actually the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:08.760 --> 00:29:14.100
first point in my life that I discovered, "Hey,&nbsp;
it's really fun to sit down and write something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:14.940 --> 00:29:20.640
just because you enjoy it, not because it's a&nbsp;
term paper or whatever." So that went on for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:20.640 --> 00:29:27.600
about a year and I don't think the "readership"&nbsp;
was probably never more than a dozen people. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:27.600 --> 00:29:33.540
then something strange started happening, which&nbsp;
is, people that I had never met started reading&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:33.540 --> 00:29:37.500
it and commenting on it, and I have no idea how&nbsp;
these people found me or where they came from.&nbsp;

00:29:38.280 --> 00:29:46.380
And that was a little bit mind blowing to me.&nbsp;
And it was around that time I started to get very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:46.380 --> 00:29:51.360
curious — so this also coincides, I was out just&nbsp;
out of school, the Great Recession was going on,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:51.360 --> 00:29:55.080
so there were no jobs anywhere. I was&nbsp;
basically living on a friend's couch.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:55.860 --> 00:30:01.140
And it kind of opened up this idea of,&nbsp;
"Hey, there's internet stuff you can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:01.140 --> 00:30:09.360
do. This blogging thing is actually a thing.&nbsp;
You can promote products and get commissions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:09.360 --> 00:30:15.540
you can put ads on your site. You can make a&nbsp;
little bit of chump change, if you think about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:15.540 --> 00:30:22.221
it and are strategic about it." And this story's&nbsp;
going to lead to you, Tim, I got to warn you.&nbsp;

00:30:22.221 --> 00:30:26.280
Tim Ferriss: Uh-oh. Uh-oh.
Mark Manson: So I started getting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:26.280 --> 00:30:32.280
curious about, I guess, you would call it kind of&nbsp;
the online marketing, online blogging world. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:33.300 --> 00:30:38.040
it was around that time I discovered The 4-Hour&nbsp;
Workweek. I think it had just come out or been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:38.040 --> 00:30:40.440
out for a year or something.
Tim Ferriss: That was April&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:40.440 --> 00:30:48.000
2007 it came out. Seems like a lifetime ago.
Mark Manson: I found that and I read it. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:48.000 --> 00:30:54.840
to your credit, I read that book and it blew open&nbsp;
my mind in terms of what the possibilities were.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:54.840 --> 00:31:01.440
I was just like, "Oh, my God, you could do all&nbsp;
sorts of shit online and automate this stuff. Oh,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:01.440 --> 00:31:05.760
my God, I'm going to be living in Argentina&nbsp;
and this is going to be great." I'm like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:05.760 --> 00:31:10.667
"Sign me up, dude. Here, I'll go buy..."
Tim Ferriss: Argentina, here I come.&nbsp;

00:31:10.667 --> 00:31:15.720
Mark Manson: Exactly. I had a very love-hate&nbsp;
experience with that book, but in hindsight,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:15.720 --> 00:31:21.840
both were good. The love side of it was — it&nbsp;
just opened my mind to so many possibilities.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:21.840 --> 00:31:32.040
The hate side of it was, I thought it was going&nbsp;
to be so much easier. The way you described some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:32.040 --> 00:31:37.920
of those examples, I'm like, "Oh, yes, this was a&nbsp;
month tops." Meanwhile, a year and a half later,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:37.920 --> 00:31:45.600
I'm still grinding 12 hours a day. I went&nbsp;
through a period of, "God damn that Tim Ferriss,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:45.600 --> 00:31:49.440
what the hell was I thinking?" You and&nbsp;
I have had a complicated relationship,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:49.440 --> 00:31:53.280
you're not aware of most of it, but —
Tim Ferriss: Oh, tell me.&nbsp;

00:31:53.280 --> 00:32:00.840
Mark Manson: No, I mean, it was a&nbsp;
difficult but very, very rewarding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:01.440 --> 00:32:07.500
period in terms of just trying all&nbsp;
these different websites, blogs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:09.840 --> 00:32:16.140
SEO pages, and AdSense pages, and 90 percent of&nbsp;
it doesn't work. Then, the 10 percent that works,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:17.700 --> 00:32:22.200
it brings in $100 a month and you're like,&nbsp;
"Okay, well if I can just do 10 more of those,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:22.200 --> 00:32:28.980
maybe that gets me somewhere." It's one of&nbsp;
those grinds that you look back with some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:28.980 --> 00:32:33.360
nostalgia and romance — there's a little&nbsp;
bit of romance to it. I don't think I'd&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:33.360 --> 00:32:38.880
ever be able to stomach it today, but as a&nbsp;
broke 25-year-old, I'm really proud of it.&nbsp;

00:32:38.880 --> 00:32:42.647
Tim Ferriss: What was the website,&nbsp;
or the primary websites at the time?&nbsp;

00:32:42.647 --> 00:32:48.480
Mark Manson: Oh, man. Back then, Google was&nbsp;
rewarding blogs. The idea was you build up a blog,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:48.480 --> 00:32:53.580
