element that has also worked tremendously well.
But before we get to that, what are the things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:42.240 --> 00:52:51.120
that you most struggle with now, or recently?
Mark Manson: This is going to be a bit of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:51.120 --> 00:52:57.540
blasé answer, especially probably for you&nbsp;
and your audience. I have always struggled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:57.540 --> 00:53:05.160
with the physical health side of things. I spent&nbsp;
pretty much all of my twenties getting my head&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:07.500 --> 00:53:13.260
in order, getting my baggage cleaned out, fixing&nbsp;
my relationships, understanding my emotions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:13.260 --> 00:53:17.160
developing self-awareness, all these&nbsp;
things. I was always so focused on that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:18.360 --> 00:53:25.320
that I never really developed good habits&nbsp;
around taking care of myself. I'm at that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:25.320 --> 00:53:31.680
point now in my late thirties where you actually&nbsp;
pay for those bad habits, because when you're 28&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:32.820 --> 00:53:37.260
you don't really pay for it, but these days — I&nbsp;
hit a point a year or two ago where I realized&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:37.260 --> 00:53:41.640
it's never going to get easier or better. It's&nbsp;
not a sexy answer, it's kind of a blah answer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:41.640 --> 00:53:46.260
but the last year especially has been&nbsp;
very, very health focused. I've actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:46.260 --> 00:53:52.920
been listening to a shit load of your episodes&nbsp;
on subjects like that. It's been very helpful.&nbsp;

00:53:54.000 --> 00:53:59.280
Tim Ferriss: I appreciate that. The&nbsp;
health piece, this is — I'm 45 now, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:00.240 --> 00:54:06.900
all of those little decisions add up. I've&nbsp;
been pretty good at maintenance, but I've also,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:06.900 --> 00:54:15.420
just through pure life aggression, sustained&nbsp;
30-plus fractures, a handful of surgeries,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:15.420 --> 00:54:27.780
and holy shit do those start to reappear.&nbsp;
The degree of self-care, what I've realized,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:27.780 --> 00:54:35.040
just needs to go up in a very non-linear fashion&nbsp;
the older you get, or you just start sliding in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:35.040 --> 00:54:42.720
a really significant way. I'm in maybe a similar&nbsp;
boat in the sense that I'm also, in the last year&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:42.720 --> 00:54:48.600
and certainly for this upcoming year, going to be&nbsp;
focusing on the health component because it's this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:49.440 --> 00:54:57.300
upstream variable that just affects positively or&nbsp;
negatively everything else. For what it's worth,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:57.300 --> 00:55:00.840
I'm happy to chat about that anytime.
Mark Manson: Thanks, man, I appreciate that.&nbsp;

00:55:00.840 --> 00:55:06.780
Tim Ferriss: Whenever I feel like I'm getting off&nbsp;
the rails, it's just, for what it's worth, I have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:06.780 --> 00:55:11.520
found — I have some degenerative disc issues in my&nbsp;
back also, which is unfortunate, but congenital–&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:12.720 --> 00:55:19.740
Kettlebell swings, slow carb diet, perhaps&nbsp;
also skipping one or two meals, let's just say&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:19.740 --> 00:55:26.520
breakfast because I find that easier than portion&nbsp;
control, and some type of core work, whether it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:27.060 --> 00:55:34.080
something like really technical Pilates or&nbsp;
let's just say gymnastic strength training also,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:34.080 --> 00:55:37.200
but that's a bit more aggressive on&nbsp;
the joints so you have to have that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:39.660 --> 00:55:47.520
stability/connective tissue strength. But if I&nbsp;
just do core work, kettlebell swings, pushups,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:47.520 --> 00:55:56.640
and some type of diet that restricts high&nbsp;
caloric density, carbohydrates to some extent,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:58.560 --> 00:56:02.700
it really makes a huge difference. Do you&nbsp;
have pains? Do you have a lot of pains?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:03.360 --> 00:56:06.420
In terms of symptoms, is that...?
Mark Manson: I actually don't,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:07.320 --> 00:56:16.140
I'm very fortunate in that regard. I just&nbsp;
got fat. Especially, I mean we're going to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:16.140 --> 00:56:21.180
get to my run the last five years and&nbsp;
all the projects I did, I overworked,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:21.180 --> 00:56:28.367
I overdid it. The wake-up call for me was actually&nbsp;
— I was writing three books at the same time.&nbsp;

00:56:28.367 --> 00:56:36.060
Tim Ferriss: Sorry.
Mark Manson: You know how stupid that is.&nbsp;

00:56:36.060 --> 00:56:42.120
I was writing three books at the same time. I&nbsp;
was living in New York, traveling, I'm on planes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:42.840 --> 00:56:48.420
if not every week, every other week. I was&nbsp;
actually sitting in my office one morning and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:48.420 --> 00:56:54.540
I started having intense heart pain, or chest pain&nbsp;
I should say. I started having intense chest pain,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:54.540 --> 00:57:02.100
and it freaked me out. I have a history of&nbsp;
heart disease in my family and I was like, "I'm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:02.100 --> 00:57:08.820
fucking 37, this can't be happening." I sat down,&nbsp;
went to a doctor, ran all the tests, everything's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:08.820 --> 00:57:13.440
fine. But he looked at me, he's like, "Have you&nbsp;
had any stress going on in your work lately?"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:16.140 --> 00:57:22.200
But yes, I had gained probably 25,&nbsp;
30 pounds. The little exercise I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:22.200 --> 00:57:25.980
had been doing before then went out the&nbsp;
window, my eating habits were terrible,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:25.980 --> 00:57:29.820
my sleeping habits were terrible, I&nbsp;
had massive circles under my eyes.&nbsp;

00:57:32.340 --> 00:57:37.260
I kind of had just this come to Jesus moment&nbsp;
of like, "Dude, you can't put this off any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:37.260 --> 00:57:41.160
longer. You have to do something to take&nbsp;
care of it." There was also a little bit of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:42.300 --> 00:57:50.220
a humbling that had to happen of realizing that&nbsp;
after a number of years of trying to do it myself,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:50.220 --> 00:57:55.440
that I just — for whatever reason, this&nbsp;
part of my life, I don't seem to have good&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:56.220 --> 00:58:00.420
decision control, so I hired a&nbsp;
coach and that was super helpful.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:04.140 --> 00:58:10.020
Speaking of that snowball effect, the further I&nbsp;
go down the path, the more I want to go down the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:10.020 --> 00:58:18.900
path. I also feel a little bit stupid because my&nbsp;
whole thing for 15 years now has been all about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:20.160 --> 00:58:26.640
how people think, feel, and helping them deal&nbsp;
with struggles and pain in their life better, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:26.640 --> 00:58:33.060
I'm sitting here and I'm like, "Wow, not drinking&nbsp;
for a few weeks, going to bed at 10:00 PM, I feel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:33.060 --> 00:58:39.360
20 percent better on a day-to-day basis. How did&nbsp;
I not realize this before?" How am I so late to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:39.360 --> 00:58:44.160
this party? It's been an interesting experience.
Tim Ferriss: Just out of curiosity, where were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:44.160 --> 00:58:49.620
you when you stopped drinking and started going&nbsp;
to bed earlier, getting a solid night's sleep,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:49.620 --> 00:58:52.127
just geographically, where were you?
Mark Manson: L.A.&nbsp;

00:58:52.127 --> 00:58:57.720
Tim Ferriss: L.A. I ask, because New York,&nbsp;
in my experience — I grew up on Long Island,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:57.720 --> 00:59:01.740
I've spent a ton of time in New York&nbsp;
City. It is just a cultural norm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:04.620 --> 00:59:12.660
I mean to drink four or five nights a&nbsp;
week, and you get on this caffeine/booze&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:12.660 --> 00:59:20.940
seesaw, which is the norm for millions&nbsp;
of people every week, if not every&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:20.940 --> 00:59:27.300
day. That's why I was asking about the switch.
Mark Manson: I was completely unprepared for how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:27.300 --> 00:59:32.700
influential moving from New York to L.A. would be&nbsp;
in that factory, because you're absolutely right,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:32.700 --> 00:59:37.260
all social life in New York revolves&nbsp;
around eating and drinking alcohol, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:38.880 --> 00:59:44.760
after seven years it adds up. It&nbsp;
really adds up. Whereas in L.A.,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:46.560 --> 00:59:51.000
people don't drink nearly as much. Everybody wants&nbsp;
to go on a hike, they want to surf, they want to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:52.680 --> 00:59:59.220
go hang out on the beach. So, it's been very good.
Tim Ferriss: The where of health matters a lot,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:59.220 --> 01:00:04.620
not just the how of health. The how of&nbsp;
health is often a consequence of the where,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:04.620 --> 01:00:12.300
in a way. L.A. does have that in&nbsp;
spades. If you haven't been to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:14.220 --> 01:00:21.060
the Venice Boardwalk green on, I want to say&nbsp;
a Sunday when the acrobats go to train, you&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:21.060 --> 01:00:27.540
should definitely check that out. You see Cirque&nbsp;
du Soleil athletes practicing on this green,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:27.540 --> 01:00:30.523
which is probably 30 by 30 feet, and —
Mark Manson: That's awesome.&nbsp;

01:00:30.523 --> 01:00:36.180
Tim Ferriss: — if you just want a casual pass&nbsp;
by a Cirque du Soleil performance, you can&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:36.180 --> 01:00:42.240
take a look at that. It's inspiring. It's really,&nbsp;
really inspiring. Let's talk about the five-year&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:42.240 --> 01:00:50.100
period of projects and the three&nbsp;
books at once. Oh, my God. Oh, boy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:50.100 --> 01:00:58.800
that's a lot. Could you describe what you did with&nbsp;
Will Smith, how it came to be, and your personal&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:00.540 --> 01:01:04.080
primary lessons learned? It doesn't&nbsp;
have to be from the content of the book,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:04.080 --> 01:01:09.240
it could also be from the interactions, exposure&nbsp;
to L.A., entertainment, et cetera. Just love&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:09.240 --> 01:01:14.400
to know what you did, for people who don't&nbsp;
know, how it happened; maybe those are in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:14.400 --> 01:01:21.240
opposite order. And then what your personal&nbsp;
primary lessons learned were from that.&nbsp;

01:01:21.240 --> 01:01:26.460
Mark Manson: I co-authored Will Smith's memoir,&nbsp;
which came out last year. It's called Will.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:27.660 --> 01:01:35.580
We worked on it for I think about two and a&nbsp;
half years together. The way it came about&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:35.580 --> 01:01:39.960
was his team actually reached out to me. He&nbsp;
had wanted to do a book for a little while.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:43.020 --> 01:01:49.080
I wasn't there, but from what I understand, he had&nbsp;
some casual meetings with a few different authors&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:51.240 --> 01:01:57.780
just to see what was out there, and when&nbsp;
they contacted me about it, first of all,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:57.780 --> 01:02:01.620
another story of my agent being great. My agent&nbsp;
didn't tell me about it right away. The first&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:01.620 --> 01:02:07.080
thing she did is she sent me an email and she&nbsp;
said, "Hypothetical question. If a celebrity&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:07.080 --> 01:02:12.960
wanted to write a book with you, on what terms&nbsp;
would you be interested?" Which is smart,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:12.960 --> 01:02:15.420
because she didn't tell me who it was.
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, that's smart.&nbsp;

01:02:15.420 --> 01:02:22.200
Mark Manson: And I said, "It would have to be&nbsp;
an A-lister, and they would have to want it to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:22.200 --> 01:02:29.280
be more than just about their life. They would&nbsp;
want to dig into their problems and history and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:29.820 --> 01:02:35.700
baggage, et cetera." Which is, as it so&nbsp;
happens, exactly what Will wanted to do.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:38.040 --> 01:02:47.640
After that, we set up the meeting. Super&nbsp;
surreal. I always tell people the craziest part&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:48.540 --> 01:02:53.460
about meeting a really, really famous person&nbsp;
isn't actually the really, really famous person,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:53.460 --> 01:02:58.380
it's all the shit that goes on around the&nbsp;
super-famous person. It's hard to explain.&nbsp;

01:02:58.380 --> 01:03:04.920
But next thing I know, I'm getting whisked away&nbsp;
to Atlanta, where I'm supposed to meet him on set&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:05.520 --> 01:03:11.340
of a film that he's working on. Turns out&nbsp;
Ang Lee is directing that film. So, I'm like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:11.340 --> 01:03:16.500
"Don't fanboy at Ang Lee, don't fanboy at Will&nbsp;
Smith," like, "Oh, God, this is so stressful."&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:16.500 --> 01:03:22.140
And you're supposed to just act casual, like this&nbsp;
is all normal. So, he finishes, they're rehearsing&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:22.140 --> 01:03:28.620
a scene or something, and I'm walking in circles,&nbsp;
not knowing where you're supposed to go, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:29.520 --> 01:03:32.640
his people are like, "Are you okay?"&nbsp;
And I'm like, "Where should I sit?&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:32.640 --> 01:03:36.240
I've never been on a Hollywood set before."
Tim Ferriss: "What do I with my hands? What&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:36.240 --> 01:03:41.280
do I do with my hands?" Like Ricky Bobby.
Mark Manson: I told one of his managers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:41.280 --> 01:03:44.940
"I've never been on a Hollywood set&nbsp;
before. I don't know if I'm in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:44.940 --> 01:03:50.160
right place or not," and she just looked at&nbsp;
me like, "Really?" And I was like, "Sorry."&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:51.720 --> 01:03:56.640
Next thing I know, they finish rehearsing.&nbsp;
We go straight to an airstrip, I'm getting&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:56.640 --> 01:04:01.080
loaded onto a private jet, and they're like, "No,&nbsp;
no, no, you need to sit in the front with Will,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:01.080 --> 01:04:07.980
because you're going to talk about the book&nbsp;
now." And I'm like, "Now? Okay." First time&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:07.980 --> 01:04:11.400
on a jet. So, we're being whisked away. The&nbsp;
whole thing is just surreal and strange,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:11.400 --> 01:04:16.140
and everywhere he goes there's a mob of&nbsp;
fans waiting for him, people shrieking.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:18.120 --> 01:04:24.480
It took a few days to get over that, get in a room&nbsp;
with him privately where it's quiet, and actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:24.480 --> 01:04:33.180
have an extended one-on-one conversation with him.
Before I went to meet him, I had two conditions&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:33.180 --> 01:04:37.800
for myself. The first one is,&nbsp;
he can't be an asshole, because&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:39.960 --> 01:04:45.360
no amount of money, no amount of prestige is worth&nbsp;
spending three years working with an asshole.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:46.560 --> 01:04:53.700
And then two was, everything needs to be&nbsp;
on the table. He needs to be very willing&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:53.700 --> 01:04:59.460
to open up and go anywhere. We had a few&nbsp;
really good, productive conversations,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:59.460 --> 01:05:06.840
and I was blown away. The thing that&nbsp;
really knocked my socks off about him&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:07.620 --> 01:05:11.520
is, he's a smart guy, obviously, he's&nbsp;
a very hard-working guy, obviously.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:12.420 --> 01:05:20.940
His charisma and his ability to read a room and&nbsp;
react in a way that makes people feel comfortable,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:22.440 --> 01:05:27.120
and then also talking to him a little bit about&nbsp;
some of the hurdles that he went through in&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:27.120 --> 01:05:34.020
his life, his ability to adapt to setback, to&nbsp;
reframe it in a way that is helpful to himself,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:34.620 --> 01:05:42.720
it's really incredible. I know this seems&nbsp;
strange to say after the Oscars incident,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:42.720 --> 01:05:46.920
but he really is one of the most emotionally&nbsp;
intelligent people that I've been around.&nbsp;

01:05:48.660 --> 01:05:54.960
We had a dinner the last night, and he just out&nbsp;
of the blue was like, "What's up, Mark? Are we&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:54.960 --> 01:05:58.920
going to do a book or what?" And I was like,&nbsp;
"Well, I've got an outline in mind," and I&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:01.500 --> 01:06:05.280
told him the thing I just said about&nbsp;
emotions and his ability to use his&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:05.280 --> 01:06:09.300
emotions to adapt to things. I also said it's&nbsp;
probably why he's such a good actor, is that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:10.020 --> 01:06:15.240
he's able to summon emotions and play with&nbsp;
them and react to them in a masterful way.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:16.260 --> 01:06:20.700
And I said, "I think the book should be built&nbsp;
around emotion. It starts with fear. It moves&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:20.700 --> 01:06:24.600
through all your defense mechanisms into&nbsp;
your fame, into all the success, until&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:25.440 --> 01:06:32.700
that facade collapsing, and it eventually,&nbsp;
finally ends up at love, at a very genuine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:32.700 --> 01:06:36.780
authentic love," which is something&nbsp;
that it took him a long time to get to.&nbsp;

01:06:38.100 --> 01:06:43.140
And I said, "As you move through all those&nbsp;
emotions, there's a word for somebody who's&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:43.140 --> 01:06:48.240
able to move through setbacks and deal with&nbsp;
any sort of negative emotion and harness it&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:49.260 --> 01:06:54.180
to advantage them. It's Will. So&nbsp;
that should be the name of the book."&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:55.320 --> 01:07:00.600
It was like the Fresh Prince came back. He was&nbsp;
just like, "Hell, yeah." He gets up, starts&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:00.600 --> 01:07:05.880
slamming stuff on the table. He's like, "Hell,&nbsp;
yeah. Hell, yeah, we're doing that." And I'm like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:05.880 --> 01:07:13.667
"All right. I got the gig?" But it was a —
Tim Ferriss: Good meeting, good meeting.&nbsp;

01:07:13.667 --> 01:07:23.160
Mark Manson: Yeah, a good meeting, good meeting.&nbsp;
But it was a wild and fun two years with him.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:28.680 --> 01:07:39.480
As you can imagine, I get so many questions&nbsp;
since March about him, but honestly he's such a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:39.480 --> 01:07:44.280
great guy deep down. Obviously, he makes mistakes&nbsp;
and does stupid things like the rest of us. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:45.840 --> 01:07:52.380
I have nothing but gratitude for that project.&nbsp;
It was very easy to write, it was very enjoyable.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:52.380 --> 01:07:59.220
The way we did was, I presented the outline, we&nbsp;
refined it together. I wrote the first draft,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:00.180 --> 01:08:05.640
he did a revision of that, and then me and&nbsp;
the editor at Penguin did a final pass.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:07.320 --> 01:08:13.560
So, it really is both of us in that book.&nbsp;
A lot of people assume it was all me,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:13.560 --> 01:08:19.200
or assume it was all him, I don't know. But it&nbsp;
really was both of us. He put a lot of work and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:19.200 --> 01:08:24.360
effort into it, and he really cared about it.
Tim Ferriss: I'm going to ask about what&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:24.360 --> 01:08:29.100
you learned about Hollywood and entertainment&nbsp;
through the process of interacting with him and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:29.820 --> 01:08:35.880
writing the book. I'll just open a door&nbsp;
if you want to walk through it; if not,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:35.880 --> 01:08:40.080
we can skip it. I know you just mentioned&nbsp;
a lot of people have asked you about the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:40.080 --> 01:08:44.640
Oscars and Chris Rock incident and so on. Is&nbsp;
there anything you want to say about that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:44.640 --> 01:08:50.940
or about him with respect to that, or&nbsp;
not? You can also just say, "Nope, not&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:50.940 --> 01:08:55.680
really," and we can move on. Totally up to you.
Mark Manson: I publicly wrote a piece about it&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:56.520 --> 01:09:06.180
as a newsletter. It's on my website. The TLDR of&nbsp;
it is basically he did something really stupid&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:06.180 --> 01:09:12.120
and unacceptable, but as somebody who spent&nbsp;
a lot of time around him, and not just him:&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:12.120 --> 01:09:17.220
his family, his friends, getting to know&nbsp;
his kids, getting to know his brother,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:20.700 --> 01:09:26.940
honestly, there is so much generosity that happens&nbsp;
with him that nobody ever sees, and it's very much&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:26.940 --> 01:09:35.220
by design. He doesn't feel a need to publicize&nbsp;
it. Just seeing what I've seen being around him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:36.840 --> 01:09:44.100
I told people, "Look, that's not&nbsp;
the real him. That's the worst&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:44.100 --> 01:09:47.640
side of him. The Will that you&nbsp;
know and that you hope is real,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:49.260 --> 01:09:56.280
that's the real Will. He's a very&nbsp;
great, loving, but flawed human being."&nbsp;

01:09:58.380 --> 01:10:05.460
In terms of what I learned, and you asked earlier&nbsp;
what I learned from him, it's funny, because he&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:05.460 --> 01:10:11.460
does have a marvelous story of resilience and&nbsp;
overcoming. He comes from a very dysfunctional&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:11.460 --> 01:10:16.560
family: an abusive father, grew up on the&nbsp;
streets of West Philly, very rough neighborhoods.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:18.420 --> 01:10:26.460
It's actually very remarkable how he navigated&nbsp;
that and avoided all the pitfalls that come&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:26.460 --> 01:10:30.420
with that sort of upbringing to get where he&nbsp;
is today. It's definitely not an accident.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:32.580 --> 01:10:39.120
That story is amazing, but there&nbsp;
were no epiphanies for me personally.&nbsp;

01:10:39.120 --> 01:10:43.380
The epiphanies for me personally were&nbsp;
just being around him as a professional;&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:44.100 --> 01:10:48.960
being around a guy like that who's been at the&nbsp;
top of his game, top of his field for 30 years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:48.960 --> 01:10:55.920
one of the most famous human beings on&nbsp;
the planet. I was blown away at the way he&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:56.760 --> 01:11:02.100
worked with his team, the way he was with&nbsp;
his fans, the way he dealt with the media.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:04.020 --> 01:11:08.640
To be honest, dude, the first time I hung out&nbsp;
with him and I saw him get mobbed by fans,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:08.640 --> 01:11:14.460
my immediate reaction was like, "Man, I should&nbsp;
be nicer to my fans." I think Will is nicer to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:14.460 --> 01:11:20.460
his fans than I am, and he's Will Smith.
Tim Ferriss: He's paddling out through&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:20.460 --> 01:11:27.120
100-foot waves every time he steps out of a door.
Mark Manson: But one of the things that he helped&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:27.120 --> 01:11:35.580
me see, and he said this to me too, is he was&nbsp;
like, "I'm very aware that every encounter I have&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:36.180 --> 01:11:43.200
with a fan is a very asymmetric encounter. For me,&nbsp;
it's five seconds that I'm going to forget about&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:45.120 --> 01:11:49.320
within a minute. For them, it's five seconds&nbsp;
they're going to remember for the rest of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:49.320 --> 01:11:55.320
their lives." So he's like, "Just put in the&nbsp;
effort for those five seconds. The payoff&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:55.320 --> 01:12:02.940
is so monumental for the other person that&nbsp;
there's no ethical argument to not do it."&nbsp;

01:12:05.340 --> 01:12:10.080
The other thing that he said that was really&nbsp;
profound for me, because as you can imagine, he's&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:10.080 --> 01:12:15.480
got this full staff of people that just follow&nbsp;
him around the world; he's got cooks and chefs and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:16.680 --> 01:12:22.080
wardrobe people and security&nbsp;
guys and everything, logistics.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:23.280 --> 01:12:30.600
I asked him once, "Do you ever feel like it's&nbsp;
weird just having this team of people that just&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:30.600 --> 01:12:34.980
shadow you everywhere you go?" I was like,&nbsp;
"When was the last time you cooked a meal&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:34.980 --> 01:12:39.540
for yourself?" He started laughing and he's like,&nbsp;
"I don't know, man, probably at least 10 years."&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:41.280 --> 01:12:47.160
But he told me, "Look, it's really&nbsp;
simple. I am world-class at one thing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:47.760 --> 01:12:56.520
and it'd be very easy for me to be the humble guy&nbsp;
and cook a meal for myself and send the chef home,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:57.120 --> 01:13:02.880
but I'm only world-class at one thing, and that&nbsp;
would take away from my time and energy from&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:02.880 --> 01:13:07.740
being world-class at that one thing. Not only&nbsp;
that, but my chef is world-class at one thing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:08.280 --> 01:13:13.620
and by sending him home, I am taking&nbsp;
away from his ability to be world-class&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:13.620 --> 01:13:21.360
at the thing that he's world-class at."
Tim Ferriss: That's cool. I saw potentially the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:21.360 --> 01:13:29.820
first part coming, but not the second part. That's&nbsp;
a significant addition, looking through the mirror&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:29.820 --> 01:13:35.400
from the other direction, not just your direction.
Mark Manson: He described it as robbing him of an&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:35.400 --> 01:13:41.280
opportunity to be excellent, which just&nbsp;
to me is such a beautiful — it's funny,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:41.280 --> 01:13:48.180
because you hear so many outsourcing conversations&nbsp;
in terms of business of how to hire and outsource&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:48.180 --> 01:13:52.020
and automate and do all this stuff. He&nbsp;
spoke about it in ethical terms, which&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:53.640 --> 01:13:55.860
was really profound for me.
Tim Ferriss:&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:57.300 --> 01:14:02.760
What did you observe, or what stood out&nbsp;
to you about how he worked with his team&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:03.780 --> 01:14:08.700
outside of that example that you just gave?
Mark Manson: This validates what we were&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:08.700 --> 01:14:14.580
talking about earlier. The first thing&nbsp;
that really stood out to me about his team&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:15.540 --> 01:14:24.900
was they've all been with him forever. It&nbsp;
was funny, he's got one guy named Scotty,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:24.900 --> 01:14:28.440
who I love. Scotty has been in a few of&nbsp;
his YouTube videos; lovely, lovely guy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:30.360 --> 01:14:34.380
Scotty was joking with me. He's like, "I'm the new&nbsp;
guy on the team. I've only been here nine years."&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:36.360 --> 01:14:40.980
Everybody in significant roles in his&nbsp;
team have been with him since at least&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:40.980 --> 01:14:46.500
the Fresh Prince days, many of them since&nbsp;
the '80s, some of them since he was a kid.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:48.120 --> 01:14:52.320
They're childhood friends that he&nbsp;
put into the appropriate place.&nbsp;

01:14:54.720 --> 01:15:00.480
That really blew me away. It also validated&nbsp;
a lot of my beliefs around loyalty and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:01.080 --> 01:15:08.160
trust. He also explained it to me once&nbsp;
as, "Look, when you become famous,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:09.300 --> 01:15:13.860
nothing is scarce. If you want money, you&nbsp;
can go get money. If you want parties,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:13.860 --> 01:15:18.060
you can go get parties. If you want sex, you&nbsp;
can go get sex. The one thing that becomes&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:18.060 --> 01:15:27.660
more scarce as you become famous is trust." So he&nbsp;
said, "I very much tried to build my organization&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:27.660 --> 01:15:33.420
to optimize for trust first and everything&nbsp;
else second," which I thought was very wise&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:34.200 --> 01:15:40.380
of him. But in terms of just operationally,&nbsp;
he's very good at including everybody.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:44.220 --> 01:15:50.880
There's no rigid hierarchy of, "Well, you're the&nbsp;
assistant of this manager, so you shouldn't be in&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:50.880 --> 01:15:56.760
the room talking about these ideas." He loves&nbsp;
to just joke and bounce creative ideas around,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:56.760 --> 01:16:04.200
and if it's the lowly production assistant&nbsp;
who comes back with a great idea, he's happy&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:04.200 --> 01:16:12.180
with that. He'll run with it. He doesn't feel a&nbsp;
possessiveness around who gets to tell him what.&nbsp;

01:16:13.080 --> 01:16:18.060
Tim Ferriss: Fascinating.&nbsp;
Super, super fascinating.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:20.400 --> 01:16:28.200
What are your goals, desires, wants maybe? I don't&nbsp;
know how crazy we want to get. But in the next,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:29.880 --> 01:16:33.780
you can choose the timeframe, three years&nbsp;
— because there was a point, as you said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:33.780 --> 01:16:40.560
you're writing for your friends&nbsp;
in college, readership per se of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:41.160 --> 01:16:46.680
no more than a dozen people, not really thinking&nbsp;
about where this is going or how you're going to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:46.680 --> 01:16:53.640
optimize it. It was just writing, having fun with&nbsp;
friends and enjoying the process. Now you have,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:55.980 --> 01:17:02.100
among other things, paradox of choice situation&nbsp;
on your hands, where there's more inbound than&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:02.100 --> 01:17:07.260
you can probably even begin to process, even&nbsp;
more than your agent can probably process.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:07.260 --> 01:17:14.220
She has to have some quick means of eliminating&nbsp;
things. How are you thinking about what you want&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:14.220 --> 01:17:21.180
to do or not do over the next handful of years?
Mark Manson: The place I'm in now, I will write&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:21.180 --> 01:17:30.960
more books, but I'm in this almost prodigal son&nbsp;
coming back to the internet moment. Doing all of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:30.960 --> 01:17:38.580
these projects over the last five years in legacy&nbsp;
media, it's been very rewarding, and it's been&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:39.120 --> 01:17:45.600
in many cases very fun and educational.&nbsp;
But what I realized is that that's not&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:49.020 --> 01:17:58.260
what I love doing. I love just the raw dynamism&nbsp;
of posting something, getting immediate feedback,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:00.120 --> 01:18:04.380
processing that feedback, understanding, "Oh,&nbsp;
people like that. Why did people like that?&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:05.520 --> 01:18:10.380
What is it about that idea that resonates with&nbsp;
people?" And then trying to figure out, "Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:10.380 --> 01:18:16.980
how could I do something more with that?" I&nbsp;
love that process so much, and the immediacy&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:16.980 --> 01:18:22.920
and the creative control that comes with that,&nbsp;
that I'm envisioning this place in my career as&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:22.920 --> 01:18:28.200
I'm coming back to that. I mentioned earlier&nbsp;
that I'm currently hiring out a video team,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:29.040 --> 01:18:34.127
and before 500 people listening to&nbsp;
this start sending me applications —&nbsp;

01:18:34.127 --> 01:18:40.020
Tim Ferriss: I can learn. I can learn, Mark.&nbsp;
I'm willing to work for free, but you have&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:40.020 --> 01:18:42.060
to teach me everything.
Mark Manson: Exactly.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:43.380 --> 01:18:48.780
I've already made the initial hires. If there&nbsp;
are more people that I'm hiring, subscribe to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:48.780 --> 01:18:57.600
my newsletter. It'll be announced there. I'm very&nbsp;
bullish on online media and independent media in&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:57.600 --> 01:19:04.380
general, and I think it has a bright future.&nbsp;
You can argue it's already the predominant&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:06.180 --> 01:19:11.160
form of media in terms of just where&nbsp;
the narratives come from and where the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:11.160 --> 01:19:14.100
best content comes from. But I think&nbsp;
that's only going to continue over the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:14.100 --> 01:19:19.920
next decade. So, I'm excited to be here for that.
There's two things that I love. One is what I just&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:19.920 --> 01:19:26.820
said, the immediacy of online content. The other&nbsp;
one is I have this very, very strong passion,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:26.820 --> 01:19:35.040
bordering on fury of a thousand suns, to take&nbsp;
the self-help world, which traditionally — when&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:35.040 --> 01:19:38.940
I was growing up, when you were growing up,&nbsp;
self-help was not something for the masses.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:40.800 --> 01:19:46.440
If you had a bunch of money in your savings and&nbsp;
could fly to a city and sit in a conference room&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:46.440 --> 01:19:53.580
for five days, that's where this content used to&nbsp;
be. I think over the past 20 years we're living&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:53.580 --> 01:20:00.240
through, I don't know if "democratizing" is the&nbsp;
right word, but just a widespread dissemination&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:00.240 --> 01:20:07.260
of these very important concepts and ideas to the&nbsp;
masses for either free or very, very, very cheap.&nbsp;

01:20:10.380 --> 01:20:13.560
I really deeply believe that&nbsp;
a lot of these concepts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:13.560 --> 01:20:17.220
these basic things that you and&nbsp;
I talk about week in, week out,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:18.360 --> 01:20:23.280
they should be everywhere. They should be taught&nbsp;
in school. Basic relationship skills should be&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:23.280 --> 01:20:28.920
taught in school. Basic meditation, a lot of&nbsp;
the exercises you do in therapy, CBT, these&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:28.920 --> 01:20:33.600
concepts should be taught everywhere. Everybody&nbsp;
should have access to them all the time. I'm very&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:33.600 --> 01:20:40.620
passionate about that idea, and that has always&nbsp;
been kind of a chip on my shoulder that I've had&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:40.620 --> 01:20:44.940
throughout my career, and it's still there.&nbsp;
There's still work to do. So, the marriage of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:44.940 --> 01:20:50.460
those two things is where my head is at right now.
Tim Ferriss: To choose the things you want to do,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:50.460 --> 01:20:56.640
which you've been describing, to home in on the&nbsp;
things that you know you enjoy. It would seem to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:56.640 --> 01:21:02.100
me that a prerequisite of ensuring that happens&nbsp;
— I have a number of follow-up questions, this is&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:02.100 --> 01:21:09.000
one — ensuring that happens requires you say no to&nbsp;
a lot of stuff that you either ignore completely,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:09.000 --> 01:21:14.040
don't respond to, or say no to a lot of stuff.&nbsp;
You're fortunate that you have a team, so Mollie&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:14.040 --> 01:21:23.040
can also say no to a lot of things on your behalf.
I'm curious how you're thinking about saying no&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:23.040 --> 01:21:29.160
to very tempting things. And they may not be&nbsp;
tempting, but I'll give you an example. You get&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:29.160 --> 01:21:33.480
an email from Mollie, she's like, "Might be worth&nbsp;
a five-minute conversation; give me a call." You&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:33.480 --> 01:21:40.800
get on the phone, she's like, "Steven Spielberg.&nbsp;
He read Will, he's not done this for anybody,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:40.800 --> 01:21:43.920
he wants you to write a three-part —
Mark Manson: Oh, God.&nbsp;

01:21:43.920 --> 01:21:47.520
Tim Ferriss: — book series."
Mark Manson: People can't see, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:47.520 --> 01:21:53.160
I'm already facepalming!.
Tim Ferriss: And maybe I'm actually screwing up&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:53.160 --> 01:21:57.240
this thought exercise by making it a three-parter.&nbsp;
It's not a three-parter, but it is going to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:58.140 --> 01:22:07.560
a year or two of focus on this, which would be&nbsp;
the seminal, definitive book with Spielberg's&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:07.560 --> 01:22:14.340
full cooperation, full access, journals,&nbsp;
everything. Pay you some insane amount of money,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:14.340 --> 01:22:19.140
twice the number that you would pick as a stretch&nbsp;
goal that you're like, "They'll never say yes to,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:19.140 --> 01:22:22.860
he's like, "I'll just pay you twice as much. Let's&nbsp;
get this over with so we can get to the work."&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:24.900 --> 01:22:28.547
What do you do when stuff like that comes&nbsp;
up? Because you know it's going to come up.&nbsp;

01:22:28.547 --> 01:22:33.120
Mark Manson: Well, I don't know if that —
Tim Ferriss: Maybe not that specifically.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:33.120 --> 01:22:42.360
I'm using an absurd goal to drive the&nbsp;
exercise, because if it's like, "Eh,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:42.360 --> 01:22:48.840
this is a B-minus offer," easy to say no to,&nbsp;
because you want to strive to do an A-plus&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:50.760 --> 01:22:58.740
in the things you just described. How do you think&nbsp;
about, say no to the stuff that will be tempting?&nbsp;

01:22:58.740 --> 01:23:03.420
Mark Manson: Well, for lower-level stuff. I&nbsp;
think the best way is to simply set rules.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:05.280 --> 01:23:10.260
I've done that with speaking the last year or two.
Tim Ferriss: What are your rules?&nbsp;

01:23:10.260 --> 01:23:14.700
Mark Manson: My rules are&nbsp;
basically it either needs to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:15.960 --> 01:23:23.940
an Uber away, or it needs to be just&nbsp;
an absolutely stupid amount of money,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:24.900 --> 01:23:30.480
which nobody takes the stupid amount of money.&nbsp;
So, it's basically just an Uber away. Also,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:30.480 --> 01:23:34.860
I decided too that I'm not really going to&nbsp;
do keynotes anymore. If you want me to sit&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:34.860 --> 01:23:40.980
onstage and answer questions, that's fine, but the&nbsp;
preparation, the thought, the practice that goes&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:40.980 --> 01:23:45.060
into a keynote, the mental energy, that sucks.
Tim Ferriss: You're not getting paid for 60&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:45.060 --> 01:23:46.367
minutes on stage.
Mark Manson: No.&nbsp;

01:23:46.367 --> 01:23:50.220
Tim Ferriss: You can pay for everything around it.
Mark Manson: Exactly, exactly. So, speaking has&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:50.220 --> 01:23:54.360
been a big one that I've cut out, and actually,&nbsp;
the delta between how speaking opportunities&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:54.360 --> 01:23:59.940
look on paper versus reality, it's just so wide,&nbsp;
because you get that email and then there's this&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:59.940 --> 01:24:04.320
number with a bunch of zeros behind it and you're&nbsp;
like, "Oh, my god, that's the easiest money ever."&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:04.320 --> 01:24:09.660
Then you actually go do it and you're like,&nbsp;
"Wow, I just spent a week not doing anything&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:09.660 --> 01:24:18.720
else to be here." So, rules around that.
To the Spielberg opportunity, my rule with&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:18.720 --> 01:24:22.800
any celebrity memoir would probably&nbsp;
be very similar to my rule with Will:&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:24.660 --> 01:24:28.860
A-lister, has to be doing it for the&nbsp;
right reasons. I think the difference&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:28.860 --> 01:24:37.620
this time would be, part of the reason I&nbsp;
gave myself chest pains a few years ago was&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:40.080 --> 01:24:45.780
I think I was so eager to say yes to things.&nbsp;
To back up a little bit, after Subtle Art,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:45.780 --> 01:24:51.300
there was a little bit of this insecurity, maybe&nbsp;
call it impostor syndrome, of like, "This might&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:51.300 --> 01:24:57.371
be my 15 minutes. Say yes to everything,&nbsp;
because who knows if this is going to come —&nbsp;

01:24:57.371 --> 01:24:59.820
Tim Ferriss: Who knows? Totally.
Mark Manson: — back around." So, I did that. I&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:59.820 --> 01:25:06.900
don't necessarily regret it, but I think this time&nbsp;
around it would be on my terms. It would be like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:08.760 --> 01:25:13.500
"Is he an A-lister?" Yes, he is. "Is he doing&nbsp;
it for the right reasons?" I'd probably have to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:13.500 --> 01:25:17.760
sit down and talk to him, get a feel for&nbsp;
that. "Is it a good fit chemistry-wise?"&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:19.620 --> 01:25:24.300
I'd have to sit down, figure that out. Then&nbsp;
I think it would be on my terms. I'd be like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:24.300 --> 01:25:30.120
"Here's my schedule for the next two years.&nbsp;
Here's where I can fit this in. This is when I'm&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:30.120 --> 01:25:37.920
available. Take it or leave it." I think the money&nbsp;
at this point would be a very, very minor factor.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:39.960 --> 01:25:43.427
I've definitely hit a —
Tim Ferriss: Only so many Skittles you can eat.&nbsp;

01:25:43.427 --> 01:25:48.180
Mark Manson: Seriously. You do hit&nbsp;
a certain number where you're like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:49.140 --> 01:25:55.500
"Fuck it, I'm just going to do stuff I&nbsp;
want to do." Very fortunate to be there.&nbsp;

01:25:56.760 --> 01:26:05.100
Tim Ferriss: Let's say Monsieur Spielberg,&nbsp;
he's like, "Great, that all sounds fantastic."&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:06.360 --> 01:26:11.580
Would it fit around what you just described&nbsp;
you would want to do, or would it start to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:11.580 --> 01:26:16.920
crowd it out, just knowing yourself?
Mark Manson: If that offer came down&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:16.920 --> 01:26:26.400
the pipeline tomorrow, my reaction would be, "I'm&nbsp;
building out a video team. We're going to start&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:26.400 --> 01:26:34.680
scaling video content in 2023. I'm under another&nbsp;
